  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>House hacking means you buy a property, live in part of it, and rent out the rest so your tenants cover most or all of your mortgage. Done right, it cuts your housing cost dramatically while you build equity in an asset you control. It is one of the most practical ways for a first-time buyer to start investing without needing a pile of cash for a separate rental.</p>

<h2>What house hacking actually looks like</h2>
<p>There is no single formula. The common versions are:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Small multifamily:</strong> buy a duplex, triplex or fourplex, live in one unit and rent the others.</li>
<li><strong>Rent by the room:</strong> buy a three- or four-bedroom house and rent spare bedrooms to roommates.</li>
<li><strong>ADU or garage apartment:</strong> live in the main house and rent a detached unit, or the reverse.</li>
<li><strong>Build it in from the start:</strong> if you are planning a new build, design a lock-off suite or a separate entrance so part of the home can be rented later.</li>
</ul>
<p>That last option is where house hacking meets home building. A floor plan with a second primary suite, its own exterior door and a kitchenette can pay you back for decades. If you are still choosing a layout, our guide on <a href="/how-to-choose-a-floor-plan-for-your-new-home/">how to choose a floor plan</a> walks through the trade-offs.</p>

<h2>Running the numbers honestly</h2>
<p>The pitch is simple: rent comes in, your payment goes out, and the gap is your real housing cost. The trap is leaving out expenses. Before you buy, build a monthly budget that includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Principal and interest on the loan</li>
<li>Property taxes (in Texas these are often the biggest surprise, so pull the actual appraisal district records)</li>
<li>Homeowners or landlord insurance, plus flood coverage if the property needs it</li>
<li>Utilities you pay as the owner</li>
<li>A repair and maintenance reserve</li>
<li>A vacancy allowance, because units do sit empty between tenants</li>
<li>Capital expenses such as roofs, HVAC and water heaters that wear out over time</li>
</ul>
<p>Use realistic rents from comparable listings near the property, not the seller's best-case figure. If the result covers everything, you are living for free. If it covers 60 to 80 percent, you are still way ahead of paying full rent somewhere else, and you are building equity.</p>
<p>The two books most investors recommend for this math are [[amz:books_house_hacking|The House Hacking Strategy]] and [[amz:books_rental_property|The Book on Rental Property Investing]]. Both walk through deal analysis in plain language.</p>
<!--products:books_house_hacking,books_rental_property,books_set_for_life|Books that teach the house hacking math-->

<h2>Financing a house hack</h2>
<p>House hacking works because you can usually buy with an owner-occupant loan, which tends to have lower down payments and better rates than an investor loan. Common options:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Loan type</th><th>Units allowed</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>FHA</td><td>1-4</td><td>Low down payment for qualified owner-occupants; mortgage insurance applies and the property must meet FHA standards.</td></tr>
<tr><td>VA</td><td>1-4</td><td>For eligible veterans and service members; often no down payment required.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conventional</td><td>1-4</td><td>Down payment requirements vary by unit count and program; may avoid long-term mortgage insurance.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Most owner-occupant loans require you to move in within a set time and intend to live there for at least a year. Lenders may let you count part of the expected rent toward qualifying income, but rules differ. Treat this as general information, not financial or lending advice, and get current terms from a lender before you make an offer. If you are building rather than buying, see our guide on <a href="/how-to-finance-building-a-house/">how to finance building a house</a>.</p>

<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ask your lender for a pre-approval that is specifically for a two- to four-unit property. Some pre-approvals are written for single-family homes only, and you do not want to discover that after a seller accepts your offer.</p></div>

<h2>Being a landlord when you live next door</h2>
<p>The financial side is the easy part. Living beside your tenants takes some structure:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Screen carefully.</strong> Use a written application, verify income and check references. Follow fair housing rules on every applicant.</li>
<li><strong>Use a written lease.</strong> Spell out rent, due dates, parking, noise, shared spaces and how repair requests are made.</li>
<li><strong>Keep it businesslike.</strong> Friendly is fine, but collect rent through an app or portal rather than knocking on doors.</li>
<li><strong>Know Texas landlord rules.</strong> The Texas Property Code sets duties on repairs, security deposits and notices. Read the basics before you sign your first lease.</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep leases, receipts and insurance papers in one place. A [[amz:document_organizer|document organizer]] for paper and a scanned backup make tax time and any insurance claim far easier.</p>

<h2>Protecting the property you live in and rent out</h2>
<p>With more people under one roof, small problems spread faster. A leaking supply line in an upstairs unit can damage two units before anyone notices. Inexpensive [[amz:water_leak|water leak sensors]] under sinks, water heaters and washing machines give early warning. A moisture meter helps you check walls after a leak and document conditions between tenants.</p>
<!--products:water_leak,moisture_meter,fireproof_safe|Gear that protects a shared property-->
<p>Talk to your insurance agent about how the policy should be written when you live in one unit and rent others. A standard homeowners policy may not fit, and being underinsured is a costly way to find out.</p>

<h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li>Buying in an area tenants do not want, just because the price is low.</li>
<li>Underestimating property taxes and insurance, which can change after purchase.</li>
<li>Skipping a professional inspection on an older multifamily building.</li>
<li>Ignoring zoning or HOA rules that limit rentals, room rentals or ADUs. Check with the city and read deed restrictions.</li>
<li>Having no cash reserve. One vacancy and one HVAC failure in the same month can sting.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rent covering your costs is never guaranteed. Markets change, and your results depend on the property, the tenants and how well you manage it.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
